  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Dharmamony vs The Food Inspector on 7 January, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Thursday, The 7Th Day Of January 2016/17Th Pousha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 3443 of 2005 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A 307/1996 Of The Ii Additional Sessions Court, Thiruvananthapuram St 80/1991 Of The Judicial First Class Magistrate Court-I, Neyyatinkara Revision Petitioners/Accused 1 & 9.: ------------------------------------ 1. Dharmamony, Secretary, Dhavala Viplava Charitable Society, Reg.No. 450, Karode Pazhayanchakkada, Kakkavila P.O. Therumalvilaveedu. 2. Dhavala Viplava Charitable Society, Reg.No. 450, Karode, Pazhanchakkada Kakkavila P.O. , Represented By A Dharmamony, Secretary. By Adv. Sri.S.Gopakumaran Nair (Sr.) Respondent(S)/Complainant And State.: ------------------------------------ 1. The Food Inspector, Neyyattinkara Cirlce, Neyyattinkara. 2. The State Of Kerala, Represented By The Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam By Public Prosecutor Sri.R.Githesh This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 07-01-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: Dharmamony vs The Food Inspector on 7 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/26504022/ 1 Scl. B. Sudheendra Kumar, J. ------------------------------------- Crl.R.P. No. 3443 of 2005 -------------------------------------- Dated this the 7th day of January, 2016",
    "facts": "3. The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- The first accused was the Secretary of Dhavala Viplava Charitable Society, Karode. The first accused was also the salesman and the board member of the ninth accused Society. Accused Nos.2 to 8 were also the board members of the society. On 28.5.1991 at about 3 p.m., PW1, the Food Inspector, Neyyattinkara purchased 900 ml. of cow milk from the first accused. After sampling as per rules, one of the samples was sent to the Public Analyst for analysis. After analysis, the report was received from the Public Analyst stating that the sample analysed did not conform to the standard prescribed for cow milk and hence the same was adulterated. On the request of the accused, the second sample was sent for analysis to the Central Food Laboratory. Ext.C1 certificate of the Central Food Laboratory would show that the Central Food Laboratory also found that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for cow milk under the PFA Rules and hence the same was adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW3 were examined and Exts.P1 to P24 series were marked for the prosecution. Ext. C1 was also marked. DW1 was examined and Ext.D1 series were marked for the revision petitioners.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.C1 certificate cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for Dharmamony vs The Food Inspector on 7 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/26504022/ 2 the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.C1 certificate cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for Dharmamony vs The Food Inspector on 7 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/26504022/ 2 the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was specified as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused."
  },
